1
00:00:06,700 --> 00:00:12,180
But noble Odysseus stayed behind in the hall,
plotting the suitors' slaughter with Athena's help.

2
00:00:12,180 --> 00:00:15,660
At once he spoke winged words to Telemachus:

3
00:00:15,660 --> 00:00:19,140
"Telemachus, the weapons of war must be laid away inside —

4
00:00:19,140 --> 00:00:25,140
all of them; and the suitors — when they miss them
and question you — talk them around with soft words:

5
00:00:25,140 --> 00:00:28,440
'I laid them out of the smoke:
they no longer looked like the arms

6
00:00:28,440 --> 00:00:35,000
Odysseus left behind when he went to Troy — they are
fouled over, as far as the breath of the fire reached.

7
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:38,900
And a greater fear — this too
a god has cast into my heart:

8
00:00:38,900 --> 00:00:43,580
that in your wine you might raise a quarrel among
you and wound one another, and shame the feast

9
00:00:43,580 --> 00:00:50,960
and the wooing. For iron of itself draws a man on.'"
So he spoke; and Telemachus obeyed his dear father,

10
00:00:50,960 --> 00:00:54,100
and calling out the nurse Eurycleia he said:

11
00:00:54,100 --> 00:00:59,480
"Nurse, come — shut the women in their quarters for me
until I have stowed away in the storeroom my father's

12
00:00:59,480 --> 00:01:03,720
beautiful arms, which the smoke
dulls, uncared for, all about

13
00:01:03,720 --> 00:01:07,560
the house, since my father went away.
I was still a child then.

14
00:01:07,560 --> 00:01:11,240
Now I want to lay them where the
breath of the fire will not reach."

15
00:01:11,240 --> 00:01:13,940
Then the dear nurse Eurycleia answered him:

16
00:01:13,940 --> 00:01:17,340
"Yes, child — and if only you would
take up the good sense at last

17
00:01:17,340 --> 00:01:20,780
to care for the house and guard all its possessions!

18
00:01:20,780 --> 00:01:23,480
But come — who then will fetch
a light and carry it for you,

19
00:01:23,480 --> 00:01:27,040
if the slave-women, who would
have lit you, may not come out?"

20
00:01:27,040 --> 00:01:29,460
Then clear-headed Telemachus answered her:

21
00:01:29,460 --> 00:01:35,840
"This stranger here. I will keep no man idle who
takes from my grain-measure, however far he has come."

22
00:01:35,840 --> 00:01:39,120
So he spoke; and for her the word stayed wingless.

23
00:01:39,120 --> 00:01:42,140
And she locked the doors of the well-settled halls.

24
00:01:42,140 --> 00:01:47,880
Then the two sprang up, Odysseus and his shining son,
and carried in the helmets and the bossed shields

25
00:01:47,880 --> 00:01:51,160
and the sharp-pointed spears;
and before them Pallas Athena,

26
00:01:51,160 --> 00:01:54,840
holding a golden lamp, made a light most beautiful.

27
00:01:54,840 --> 00:01:57,500
And suddenly Telemachus said to his father:

28
00:01:57,500 --> 00:02:00,220
"Father — here is a great wonder my eyes look on!

29
00:02:00,220 --> 00:02:06,660
The walls of the hall — no question — and the fine crossbeams,
the fir rafters, and the pillars rising high

30
00:02:06,660 --> 00:02:09,480
shine in my eyes as if from a blazing fire.

31
00:02:09,480 --> 00:02:13,560
Surely some god is in the house,
one of those who hold wide heaven."

32
00:02:13,560 --> 00:02:16,280
Then Odysseus of the many designs answered him:

33
00:02:16,280 --> 00:02:19,840
"Quiet. Rein in your thought. Ask no questions.

34
00:02:19,840 --> 00:02:22,580
This is the way of the gods who hold Olympus.

35
00:02:22,580 --> 00:02:27,600
Go you to bed. I will remain behind here to stir
the slave-women a little more, and your mother:

36
00:02:27,600 --> 00:02:30,920
in her grief she will ask me about every particular."

37
00:02:30,920 --> 00:02:36,440
So he spoke; and Telemachus went out through the hall
to his chamber, under the blazing torches, to lie down

38
00:02:36,440 --> 00:02:39,840
where he always slept when sweet sleep came to him.

39
00:02:39,840 --> 00:02:42,720
There he lay down now too, and waited for shining Dawn.

40
00:02:42,720 --> 00:02:47,580
But noble Odysseus stayed behind in the hall,
plotting the suitors' slaughter with Athena's help.

41
00:02:47,580 --> 00:02:53,340
And out of her chamber came watchful-minded
Penelope, like Artemis, or golden Aphrodite.

42
00:02:53,340 --> 00:02:57,380
They set her chair, where she
always sat, beside the fire —

43
00:02:57,380 --> 00:03:01,320
a chair wound round with ivory
and silver, which the craftsman

44
00:03:01,320 --> 00:03:05,040
Icmalius once made, and fixed beneath it a footrest

45
00:03:05,040 --> 00:03:09,020
all of a piece with it,
where a great fleece would be thrown.

46
00:03:09,020 --> 00:03:12,540
There she sat down now, watchful-minded Penelope.

47
00:03:12,540 --> 00:03:18,340
And the white-armed slave-women came in from the hall
and cleared away the piles of bread, and the tables,

48
00:03:18,340 --> 00:03:21,620
and the cups from which the overbearing men had drunk;

49
00:03:21,620 --> 00:03:24,820
they raked the fire from the
braziers to the floor and stacked

50
00:03:24,820 --> 00:03:28,460
fresh wood upon them, for light and for warmth.

51
00:03:28,460 --> 00:03:31,460
And Melantho went at Odysseus a second time:

52
00:03:31,460 --> 00:03:35,660
"Stranger — are you going to plague
us even now, all through the night,

53
00:03:35,660 --> 00:03:38,240
prowling around the house, spying on the women?

54
00:03:38,240 --> 00:03:42,180
Out the door, you wretch —
and be glad of the meal you got —

55
00:03:42,180 --> 00:03:45,820
or you'll go out quick enough
with a torch-brand at your back!"

56
00:03:45,820 --> 00:03:50,740
Then, glaring from under his brows,
Odysseus of the many designs replied:

57
00:03:50,740 --> 00:03:55,060
"Woman possessed — why go for me with a heart so angry?

58
00:03:55,060 --> 00:03:59,020
Because I am dirty, because
the clothes on my body are bad,

59
00:03:59,020 --> 00:04:02,640
because I beg through the land? Necessity is on me:

60
00:04:02,640 --> 00:04:05,880
that is what beggars and wandering men are like.

61
00:04:05,880 --> 00:04:12,560
I too once lived in a house among men, a fortunate
man in a wealthy one, and I often gave to a wanderer,

62
00:04:12,560 --> 00:04:15,940
whoever he might be and whatever need he came in.

63
00:04:15,940 --> 00:04:22,240
Slaves I had past counting, and everything in plenty
by which men live well and get the name of rich.

64
00:04:22,240 --> 00:04:26,800
But Zeus the son of Cronos stripped it all —
such, no doubt, was his will.

65
00:04:26,800 --> 00:04:29,940
So look to yourself, woman: take care you never lose

66
00:04:29,940 --> 00:04:33,820
all that shine of yours,
that sets you off among the slave-women —

67
00:04:33,820 --> 00:04:39,820
in case your mistress turns on you in anger,
or Odysseus comes. For a portion of hope is left.

68
00:04:39,820 --> 00:04:46,240
And even if he is dead as you think, and past returning,
by Apollo's grace there is his son by now,

69
00:04:46,240 --> 00:04:53,600
such a son — Telemachus. Not one woman in these halls
escapes his eye at her mischief. He is past that age."

70
00:04:53,600 --> 00:04:57,240
So he spoke; and watchful-minded Penelope heard him,

71
00:04:57,240 --> 00:05:00,440
and rounded on the handmaid,
and spoke a word, and named her:

72
00:05:00,440 --> 00:05:04,380
"Be sure of this, bold face,
dog past all shame: your grand

73
00:05:04,380 --> 00:05:08,540
performance is not lost on me —
you will wipe it off on your own head.

74
00:05:08,540 --> 00:05:11,800
You knew — every part of it —
you heard it from my own mouth:

75
00:05:11,800 --> 00:05:17,700
that I meant to question the stranger in my halls
about my husband, for whom I grieve past telling."

76
00:05:17,700 --> 00:05:20,620
Then she spoke to Eurynome the housekeeper:

77
00:05:20,620 --> 00:05:25,860
"Eurynome, bring up a bench, and a fleece on it,
for the stranger to sit and speak his word and hear me

78
00:05:25,860 --> 00:05:29,520
out: I wish to question him through and through."

79
00:05:29,520 --> 00:05:35,060
So she spoke; and the woman bustled to bring the polished
bench and set it down, and threw a fleece upon it,

80
00:05:35,060 --> 00:05:37,280
and there much-enduring noble Odysseus sat.

81
00:05:37,280 --> 00:05:41,160
And watchful-minded Penelope began to speak among them:

82
00:05:41,160 --> 00:05:44,000
"Stranger, this first will I myself ask you:

83
00:05:44,000 --> 00:05:48,320
What man are you, and from where?
Where are your city and your parents?"

84
00:05:48,320 --> 00:05:51,140
Then Odysseus of the many designs answered her:

85
00:05:51,140 --> 00:05:57,080
"Lady, no mortal man on the boundless earth could find
a fault in you: your fame goes up to the wide sky —

86
00:05:57,080 --> 00:06:02,580
like the fame of some blameless king,
a god-fearing man ruling among many people and mighty,

87
00:06:02,580 --> 00:06:05,160
upholding straight judgments; and the black earth bears

88
00:06:05,160 --> 00:06:08,820
wheat and barley under him,
and the trees hang heavy with fruit,

89
00:06:08,820 --> 00:06:11,960
the flocks throw firm young, the sea yields fish,

90
00:06:11,960 --> 00:06:15,620
all from his good governing;
and under him the people flourish.

91
00:06:15,620 --> 00:06:19,020
So — question me now on any
other matter, here in your house,

92
00:06:19,020 --> 00:06:23,760
but do not ask my race and my fathers' country,
or you will fill my heart still fuller with pains

93
00:06:23,760 --> 00:06:27,920
as I remember: I am a man of many
sorrows. And there is no need

94
00:06:27,920 --> 00:06:33,660
to sit in a stranger's house grieving and lamenting —
grief without respite makes everything worse —

95
00:06:33,660 --> 00:06:37,780
and one of the slave-women might
take offense — or you yourself —

96
00:06:37,780 --> 00:06:41,640
and say I swim in tears because
the wine has weighed my wits."

97
00:06:41,640 --> 00:06:44,340
Then watchful-minded Penelope answered him:

98
00:06:44,340 --> 00:06:50,280
"Stranger, my excellence — my face, my figure —
the deathless ones destroyed the day the Argives

99
00:06:50,280 --> 00:06:53,680
boarded ship for Ilium, and my husband Odysseus with them.

100
00:06:53,680 --> 00:07:00,040
If he came back — if he watched over this life of
mine — my fame would be greater so, and lovelier.

101
00:07:00,040 --> 00:07:04,460
Now I only grieve: so many evils the god has driven at me.

102
00:07:04,460 --> 00:07:10,960
All the great men who hold power among the islands —
Dulichium and Same and wooded Zacynthus —

103
00:07:10,960 --> 00:07:14,400
and those whose homes are round clear-skied Ithaca itself:

104
00:07:14,400 --> 00:07:17,820
they woo me against my will, and wear this house away.

105
00:07:17,820 --> 00:07:23,840
So I pay no mind to strangers, none to suppliants,
none at all to heralds, the workers for the people;

106
00:07:23,840 --> 00:07:28,420
no — longing for Odysseus melts the heart in me.

107
00:07:28,420 --> 00:07:31,980
They press the marriage on;
and I wind out my skein of tricks.

108
00:07:31,980 --> 00:07:35,160
First it was the robe: a god breathed it into my heart —

109
00:07:35,160 --> 00:07:38,540
to set up a great loom in the halls and weave at it,

110
00:07:38,540 --> 00:07:42,640
fine-threaded, beyond all measure wide;
and I told them straightway:

111
00:07:42,640 --> 00:07:48,140
'Young men, my suitors — since noble Odysseus is dead,
wait, though you burn for my wedding, till I finish

112
00:07:48,140 --> 00:07:54,400
this cloth — let my spinning not be wasted on the wind —
a shroud for the hero Laertes, against the day

113
00:07:54,400 --> 00:07:57,640
the ruinous doom of death that lays men low will take him:

114
00:07:57,640 --> 00:08:00,100
let no Achaean woman in the land hold it against me

115
00:08:00,100 --> 00:08:03,480
that a man who won so much should
lie without his winding-sheet.'

116
00:08:03,480 --> 00:08:06,620
So I spoke, and their proud hearts were persuaded.

117
00:08:06,620 --> 00:08:11,260
Then every day I wove at the great loom,
and every night I unwove it by torchlight.

118
00:08:11,260 --> 00:08:15,400
Three years my trick escaped them,
and I kept the Achaeans persuaded;

119
00:08:15,400 --> 00:08:18,720
but when the fourth year came, as the seasons rolled on,

120
00:08:18,720 --> 00:08:22,320
the months waning away,
the long tale of days accomplished,

121
00:08:22,320 --> 00:08:28,240
then — through my slave-women, those careless bitches —
they came on me and caught me, and shouted me down.

122
00:08:28,240 --> 00:08:31,600
So I finished it — against my will, under force.

123
00:08:31,600 --> 00:08:36,280
And now I can neither escape the marriage nor
find any other design. My parents urge me hard

124
00:08:36,280 --> 00:08:42,520
to marry; and my son chafes as these men eat his living —
he understands now: he is a man by now, fully able

125
00:08:42,520 --> 00:08:45,480
to care for a house that Zeus grants glory to.

126
00:08:45,480 --> 00:08:48,500
But even so, tell me your stock, where you are from:

127
00:08:48,500 --> 00:08:52,020
you were not born of the oak in
the old tales, nor of a stone."

128
00:08:52,020 --> 00:08:54,780
Then Odysseus of the many designs answered her:

129
00:08:54,780 --> 00:08:59,780
"Honored wife of Laertes' son Odysseus,
will you not leave off asking me my lineage?

130
00:08:59,780 --> 00:09:03,200
Well — I will tell you.
Though truly you hand me over to sorrows

131
00:09:03,200 --> 00:09:08,740
more than hold me now: that is the way of it,
when a man has been gone from his country as long as I have,

132
00:09:08,740 --> 00:09:11,940
wandering through many cities of men, suffering pains.

133
00:09:11,940 --> 00:09:14,900
Even so, I will tell you what you ask and probe for.

134
00:09:14,900 --> 00:09:18,320
There is a land called Crete, out in the wine-dark sea,

135
00:09:18,320 --> 00:09:23,700
beautiful and rich, ringed by the water;
and in it are people past counting, and ninety cities:

136
00:09:23,700 --> 00:09:28,700
tongue mixed with tongue — Achaeans there,
great-hearted native Cretans, Cydonians,

137
00:09:28,700 --> 00:09:32,060
Dorians in their three clans, and noble Pelasgians;

138
00:09:32,060 --> 00:09:35,260
and among the cities is Cnossus,
the great town, where Minos

139
00:09:35,260 --> 00:09:41,580
was king by nine-year terms, great Zeus's intimate —
the father of my father, great-hearted Deucalion.

140
00:09:41,580 --> 00:09:46,560
Deucalion fathered me, and lord Idomeneus;
but Idomeneus had gone in the beaked ships

141
00:09:46,560 --> 00:09:50,460
to Ilium with the sons of Atreus.
My famous name is Aethon —

142
00:09:50,460 --> 00:09:54,120
the younger brother by birth;
he was elder, and the better man.

143
00:09:54,120 --> 00:09:56,660
There I saw Odysseus, and gave him guest-gifts:

144
00:09:56,660 --> 00:09:59,400
for the strength of the wind had brought him down on Crete

145
00:09:59,400 --> 00:10:02,940
as he made for Troy, driving him off course past Malea.

146
00:10:02,940 --> 00:10:05,580
He anchored at Amnisus, by the cave of Eileithyia,

147
00:10:05,580 --> 00:10:08,920
in that difficult harbor, and barely rode out the squalls.

148
00:10:08,920 --> 00:10:12,600
He came straight up to the town, asking for Idomeneus:

149
00:10:12,600 --> 00:10:15,440
his guest-friend, he said, beloved and honored.

150
00:10:15,440 --> 00:10:18,740
But it was now the tenth dawn
for Idomeneus, or the eleventh,

151
00:10:18,740 --> 00:10:21,520
since he had gone with the beaked ships to Ilium.

152
00:10:21,520 --> 00:10:27,400
So I brought him to the house and gave him welcome,
warm and openhanded — the house had plenty in it —

153
00:10:27,400 --> 00:10:30,160
and for the rest, for the
companions who followed with him,

154
00:10:30,160 --> 00:10:33,700
I gathered barley-meal from the
public store, and sparkling wine,

155
00:10:33,700 --> 00:10:37,460
and oxen for sacrifice, till their hearts were satisfied.

156
00:10:37,460 --> 00:10:40,120
Twelve days the noble Achaeans stayed with me,

157
00:10:40,120 --> 00:10:43,380
pinned there: the great North Wind
held them, and would not let them

158
00:10:43,380 --> 00:10:46,580
stand even on shore — some hard god had raised it.

159
00:10:46,580 --> 00:10:49,860
On the thirteenth the wind dropped,
and they put out to sea."

160
00:10:49,860 --> 00:10:53,160
He made the many lies he told seem like the truth.

161
00:10:53,160 --> 00:10:57,120
And as she listened her tears ran, and her flesh melted —

162
00:10:57,120 --> 00:11:04,140
as snow melts away on the high crests of the mountains,
snow the West Wind piled and the East Wind thaws,

163
00:11:04,140 --> 00:11:07,160
and as it melts the rivers run full with it:

164
00:11:07,160 --> 00:11:10,060
so her beautiful cheeks melted with falling tears,

165
00:11:10,060 --> 00:11:15,100
as she wept for her own husband —
sitting at her side. And Odysseus

166
00:11:15,100 --> 00:11:21,080
pitied his wife in his heart as she grieved,
but his eyes stood fixed as horn or iron,

167
00:11:21,080 --> 00:11:25,280
unwavering in their lids: with craft he hid his tears.

168
00:11:25,280 --> 00:11:31,280
And when she had taken her fill of tear-heavy mourning,
she found words again, and answered him:

169
00:11:31,280 --> 00:11:34,880
"Now, stranger, I think I shall put you to the test:

170
00:11:34,880 --> 00:11:40,480
whether you truly hosted my husband there in your
halls with his godlike companions, as you say.

171
00:11:40,480 --> 00:11:43,240
Tell me what kind of clothes he wore on his body,

172
00:11:43,240 --> 00:11:47,800
and what kind of man he was,
and the companions who followed him."

173
00:11:47,800 --> 00:11:50,900
Then Odysseus of the many designs answered her:

174
00:11:50,900 --> 00:11:56,160
"Lady, that is hard — for one so long apart
to say. It is the twentieth year already

175
00:11:56,160 --> 00:11:59,320
since he went from there and left my country behind.

176
00:11:59,320 --> 00:12:03,440
Even so I will tell you how my heart's eye pictures him.

177
00:12:03,440 --> 00:12:06,560
Noble Odysseus wore a woolen mantle of sea-purple,

178
00:12:06,560 --> 00:12:10,300
a double fold; and the brooch on it was fashioned of gold,

179
00:12:10,300 --> 00:12:14,420
with paired sheaths for the pins;
the face of it was a marvel:

180
00:12:14,420 --> 00:12:20,820
a hound held a dappled fawn between its forepaws,
gripping it as it writhed. And everyone wondered at them —

181
00:12:20,820 --> 00:12:24,100
both of gold: the one gripping the fawn and throttling it,

182
00:12:24,100 --> 00:12:28,300
the fawn straining with its feet
to escape, even as it struggled.

183
00:12:28,300 --> 00:12:34,640
And I noticed the tunic on his body, all a-shimmer,
like the skin that slips from a dried onion:

184
00:12:34,640 --> 00:12:38,440
that soft it was, and bright as the sun itself.

185
00:12:38,440 --> 00:12:41,800
Many the women, I tell you, who gazed at him in wonder.

186
00:12:41,800 --> 00:12:46,020
And another thing I will tell you:
lay it up in your heart.

187
00:12:46,020 --> 00:12:49,500
I do not know whether Odysseus
wore these things from home,

188
00:12:49,500 --> 00:12:53,040
or some companion gave them to him boarding the fast ship,

189
00:12:53,040 --> 00:13:00,100
or perhaps some guest-friend, since to many men
Odysseus was dear: few of the Achaeans were his equals.

190
00:13:00,100 --> 00:13:06,540
I too gave him things: a sword of bronze,
a folded mantle, fine, sea-purple, and a fringed tunic,

191
00:13:06,540 --> 00:13:09,780
and sent him off with honor on his well-benched ship.

192
00:13:09,780 --> 00:13:15,460
Also a herald attended him, a little older than he was;
and I will tell you what he looked like too:

193
00:13:15,460 --> 00:13:22,840
round in the shoulders, dark-skinned, curly-headed,
Eurybates his name; and Odysseus prized him above

194
00:13:22,840 --> 00:13:26,600
his other companions, for their minds ran matched.

195
00:13:26,600 --> 00:13:30,220
So he spoke; and he stirred in her
still more the longing for weeping,

196
00:13:30,220 --> 00:13:33,820
as she recognized the tokens Odysseus described so surely.

197
00:13:33,820 --> 00:13:40,000
And when she had taken her fill of tear-heavy mourning,
then she found words again, and said to him:

198
00:13:40,000 --> 00:13:45,800
"Now, stranger, pitiable as you were to me before,
in my halls you shall be dear and honored.

199
00:13:45,800 --> 00:13:50,020
It was I who gave him those clothes —
I, the very clothes you speak of —

200
00:13:50,020 --> 00:13:53,100
folded from the storeroom,
and I pinned on the shining brooch

201
00:13:53,100 --> 00:13:59,360
to be his jewel. And I shall never welcome him
again come home to his own dear fatherland.

202
00:13:59,360 --> 00:14:05,500
By an evil fate Odysseus went in the hollow
ship to look on Evil-Ilium, never to be named."

203
00:14:05,500 --> 00:14:08,640
Then Odysseus of the many designs answered her:

204
00:14:08,640 --> 00:14:13,740
"Honored wife of Laertes' son Odysseus,
mar your lovely skin no more, and melt your heart

205
00:14:13,740 --> 00:14:18,480
no more away, weeping for your husband.
Not that I blame you at all:

206
00:14:18,480 --> 00:14:24,200
any woman mourns the husband she has lost,
the man of her youth, whose children she bore in love —

207
00:14:24,200 --> 00:14:28,580
even a lesser man than Odysseus,
whom they call the equal of the gods.

208
00:14:28,580 --> 00:14:31,060
But cease from weeping, and attend to my word:

209
00:14:31,060 --> 00:14:34,700
for I will speak the exact truth and hide nothing:

210
00:14:34,700 --> 00:14:40,140
I have already heard of Odysseus' homecoming —
near, in the rich land of the Thesprotian men,

211
00:14:40,140 --> 00:14:43,620
alive; and he is bringing many fine treasures home,

212
00:14:43,620 --> 00:14:47,480
begging his way through the country.
But his trusted companions

213
00:14:47,480 --> 00:14:51,780
he lost, and the hollow ship, on the wine-dark sea,

214
00:14:51,780 --> 00:14:55,420
on the way from the island of
Thrinacia: for Zeus and Helios

215
00:14:55,420 --> 00:15:00,240
were set at odds with him —
his companions killed the Sun's cattle.

216
00:15:00,240 --> 00:15:05,700
They all perished in the surging sea; but he rode
the keel of the ship, and the wave threw him ashore

217
00:15:05,700 --> 00:15:10,020
on the land of the Phaeacians,
whose birth is next to the gods'.

218
00:15:10,020 --> 00:15:15,400
They honored him from the heart like a god, and gave
him many things, and were themselves ready to bring him

219
00:15:15,400 --> 00:15:18,160
home unharmed. And Odysseus would have been here

220
00:15:18,160 --> 00:15:21,620
long since; but this seemed to
his heart the more profitable way:

221
00:15:21,620 --> 00:15:27,560
to travel wide and gather wealth — for beyond all
mortal men Odysseus knows the profitable turn:

222
00:15:27,560 --> 00:15:30,000
no living man could rival him at that.

223
00:15:30,000 --> 00:15:32,820
So Pheidon told me all of it,
the king of the Thesprotians;

224
00:15:32,820 --> 00:15:36,320
and he swore an oath to me,
pouring the libation in his house,

225
00:15:36,320 --> 00:15:42,100
that a ship was hauled down and a crew standing
ready to convey him home to his own dear fatherland.

226
00:15:42,100 --> 00:15:48,140
But me he sent off first: for a Thesprotian ship
happened to be sailing for Dulichium, rich in wheat.

227
00:15:48,140 --> 00:15:51,380
And he showed me all the treasure Odysseus had gathered:

228
00:15:51,380 --> 00:15:54,940
truly it would feed a man's line to the tenth generation,

229
00:15:54,940 --> 00:15:58,220
such a store of the lord's goods lay there in his halls.

230
00:15:58,220 --> 00:16:04,320
Odysseus, he said, was gone to Dodona, to hear the
will of Zeus out of the god's own high-crowned oak:

231
00:16:04,320 --> 00:16:10,080
how he should come home to his own dear fatherland,
openly or in secret, after so long away.

232
00:16:10,080 --> 00:16:15,100
So he is safe; so he will come — and soon now,
very near: not far from his friends and his fathers' land

233
00:16:15,100 --> 00:16:19,060
will he be for long. And still
I will give you my oath on it.

234
00:16:19,060 --> 00:16:22,760
Now Zeus be my witness first —
highest and best of the gods —

235
00:16:22,760 --> 00:16:25,640
and the hearth of blameless Odysseus,
to which I have come:

236
00:16:25,640 --> 00:16:28,840
all this shall be accomplished exactly as I say.

237
00:16:28,840 --> 00:16:32,180
Within this very turning of the
light Odysseus will be here:

238
00:16:32,180 --> 00:16:35,000
between this month's waning and the rising of the next."

239
00:16:35,000 --> 00:16:37,760
Then watchful-minded Penelope answered him:

240
00:16:37,760 --> 00:16:40,500
"Ah, stranger, if only that word might be accomplished:

241
00:16:40,500 --> 00:16:43,280
then you would quickly know my friendship, and gifts

242
00:16:43,280 --> 00:16:47,020
in plenty from me — any man who
met you would call you blessed.

243
00:16:47,020 --> 00:16:50,220
But this is how my heart forebodes it, and how it will be:

244
00:16:50,220 --> 00:16:55,000
Odysseus is not coming home; and you will get no
passage — for there are no masters in this house

245
00:16:55,000 --> 00:17:01,300
such as Odysseus was among men — if he ever was —
to welcome honored strangers, and to send them on.

246
00:17:01,300 --> 00:17:04,380
But wash him, my handmaids, and lay out a bed:

247
00:17:04,380 --> 00:17:09,060
bedstead and cloaks and shining blankets,
so he may come warm through to Dawn of the golden throne.

248
00:17:09,060 --> 00:17:12,460
And in the early morning bathe him and rub him with oil,

249
00:17:12,460 --> 00:17:16,100
so that here inside, at Telemachus' side,
he may look to his dinner,

250
00:17:16,100 --> 00:17:18,860
seated in the hall. And the worse for any man of them

251
00:17:18,860 --> 00:17:25,260
who galls him — heart-destroyer: he will accomplish
nothing here ever again, rage at it as he bitterly may.

252
00:17:25,260 --> 00:17:26,280
Whether I stand out

253
00:17:26,280 --> 00:17:31,660
at all among women in mind and thoughtful counsel,
if all unwashed and badly dressed you sit at meat

254
00:17:31,660 --> 00:17:34,580
in my halls? Men are creatures of a short day.

255
00:17:34,580 --> 00:17:40,160
The man who is hard himself, and knows hard thoughts,
on him all mortals call down pains to come

256
00:17:40,160 --> 00:17:44,380
while he lives, and when he is
dead they mock him, every one;

257
00:17:44,380 --> 00:17:48,020
but the man who is blameless himself,
and knows blameless thoughts —

258
00:17:48,020 --> 00:17:53,220
his fame the guests he served carry wide
to all mankind, and many call him noble."

259
00:17:53,220 --> 00:17:56,200
Then Odysseus of the many designs answered her:

260
00:17:56,200 --> 00:18:00,800
"Honored wife of Laertes' son Odysseus,
cloaks and shining blankets turned hateful to me

261
00:18:00,800 --> 00:18:06,240
the day I first put the snowy mountains of Crete
behind me, going aboard the long-oared ship.

262
00:18:06,240 --> 00:18:09,640
I will lie this night as I have lain
through sleepless nights before:

263
00:18:09,640 --> 00:18:14,920
many the nights I have spent on some rough bed,
waiting it out for shining fair-throned Dawn.

264
00:18:14,920 --> 00:18:17,500
Nor do foot-washings bring my heart any pleasure:

265
00:18:17,500 --> 00:18:22,380
no woman shall touch this foot of mine
of all those who serve about your house —

266
00:18:22,380 --> 00:18:26,080
unless there is some old woman, old and faithful-hearted,

267
00:18:26,080 --> 00:18:29,560
who has endured in her spirit as much as I have endured:

268
00:18:29,560 --> 00:18:33,120
such a one I would not grudge the touching of my feet."

269
00:18:33,120 --> 00:18:35,880
Then watchful-minded Penelope answered him:

270
00:18:35,880 --> 00:18:42,500
"Dear stranger — no man so discerning, of all the guests
from far lands, ever came more welcome to my house:

271
00:18:42,500 --> 00:18:45,620
so shaped and thought-through is everything you say.

272
00:18:45,620 --> 00:18:48,660
I have an old woman whose breast is packed with counsel,

273
00:18:48,660 --> 00:18:54,760
who nursed and reared that unlucky man, my husband —
took him into her arms the hour his mother bore him:

274
00:18:54,760 --> 00:18:58,660
she will wash your feet, feeble with age though she is.

275
00:18:58,660 --> 00:19:01,040
Up now, watchful-minded Eurycleia:

276
00:19:01,040 --> 00:19:04,760
wash him — a man the same age as
your master. And perhaps Odysseus

277
00:19:04,760 --> 00:19:08,080
by now is like him in his feet, like him in his hands:

278
00:19:08,080 --> 00:19:11,200
for in misfortune mortal men age fast."

279
00:19:11,200 --> 00:19:14,380
So she spoke; and the old woman hid her face in her hands

280
00:19:14,380 --> 00:19:18,180
and shed hot tears, and gave voice to words of mourning:

281
00:19:18,180 --> 00:19:21,680
"Ah me, child, and I helpless for you! Truly Zeus

282
00:19:21,680 --> 00:19:25,660
hated you beyond all men —
and yours was a god-fearing heart.

283
00:19:25,660 --> 00:19:31,980
For no mortal ever burned to Zeus who delights in
thunder so many fat thigh-pieces, such choice hecatombs,

284
00:19:31,980 --> 00:19:36,740
as you gave him, praying you might come to
a sleek old age and raise your shining son.

285
00:19:36,740 --> 00:19:40,160
And now from you alone he has
cut off the day of homecoming.

286
00:19:40,160 --> 00:19:42,620
So too, I suppose, the women mocked him —

287
00:19:42,620 --> 00:19:46,860
women of strangers in far lands,
when he came to some man's famous house —

288
00:19:46,860 --> 00:19:49,820
just as these bitches here mock you, all of them together:

289
00:19:49,820 --> 00:19:53,340
it is to dodge their insults, their crowd of taunts,

290
00:19:53,340 --> 00:19:57,440
that you will not let the women wash you.
And I — not at all unwilling —

291
00:19:57,440 --> 00:20:01,480
am ordered to it by Icarius' daughter,
watchful-minded Penelope.

292
00:20:01,480 --> 00:20:07,140
So I will wash your feet — for Penelope's own sake and
for yours as well; for the heart within me is stirred

293
00:20:07,140 --> 00:20:10,440
with sorrows. But come — take in the word I say:

294
00:20:10,440 --> 00:20:15,420
many a travel-worn stranger has come to this house,
but never, I tell you, have I seen such a likeness

295
00:20:15,420 --> 00:20:19,760
as you have — in body, in voice, in feet — to Odysseus."

296
00:20:19,760 --> 00:20:22,760
Then Odysseus of the many designs answered her:

297
00:20:22,760 --> 00:20:25,620
"Old mother, so they all say who have seen the two of us

298
00:20:25,620 --> 00:20:31,140
with their own eyes: that we are very like each
other — just as you yourself observe, and say."

299
00:20:31,140 --> 00:20:37,260
So he spoke; and the old woman took the shining
basin she used for washing feet, and poured in water,

300
00:20:37,260 --> 00:20:40,560
cold in plenty, then added hot to it. And Odysseus

301
00:20:40,560 --> 00:20:43,740
sat down at the hearth —
and swung round quickly toward the dark,

302
00:20:43,740 --> 00:20:50,120
for a fear came over his heart that as she took hold
she might mark the scar, and the whole work come open.

303
00:20:50,120 --> 00:20:53,520
She came close and began to wash
her master. And at once she knew

304
00:20:53,520 --> 00:20:56,460
the scar — the wound the boar
dealt him with its white tusk

305
00:20:56,460 --> 00:21:02,400
when he went to Parnassus, to Autolycus and his sons,
his mother's noble father, who outdid all men

306
00:21:02,400 --> 00:21:07,820
in thievery and the oath: the god himself had
given it him; to him he burned welcome thigh-pieces

307
00:21:07,820 --> 00:21:12,020
of lambs and of kids, and the
god companioned him with a will.

308
00:21:12,020 --> 00:21:18,000
Once Autolycus, coming to the rich land of Ithaca,
found his daughter's son a baby newly born;

309
00:21:18,000 --> 00:21:23,360
and Eurycleia laid the child on his knees as he
finished supper, and spoke a word, and named him:

310
00:21:23,360 --> 00:21:29,520
"Autolycus — find the name yourself, to give your
own child's child. He is a child of many prayers."

311
00:21:29,520 --> 00:21:32,040
Then Autolycus answered her and said:

312
00:21:32,040 --> 00:21:36,280
"Son-in-law and daughter, give him the name I say.

313
00:21:36,280 --> 00:21:41,880
Since I have come to this place at odds with many —
with men and with women across the nourishing earth —

314
00:21:41,880 --> 00:21:45,400
let Odysseus, Man at Odds, be the name he carries.

315
00:21:45,400 --> 00:21:49,100
And for my part: when he is grown, and comes to Parnassus,

316
00:21:49,100 --> 00:21:55,980
to his mother's great house, where my possessions are,
I will give him a share and send him home rejoicing."

317
00:21:55,980 --> 00:22:00,080
For those gifts Odysseus went:
to receive his shining presents.

318
00:22:00,080 --> 00:22:06,140
And Autolycus and the sons of Autolycus clasped his
hands in welcome, and greeted him with honeyed words;

319
00:22:06,140 --> 00:22:10,180
and Amphithea, his mother's mother,
folding Odysseus in her arms,

320
00:22:10,180 --> 00:22:13,340
kissed his head, and both his beautiful eyes.

321
00:22:13,340 --> 00:22:19,720
Then Autolycus called to his glorious sons to
make the meal ready; and they heard his urging:

322
00:22:19,720 --> 00:22:25,280
at once they led in a bull of five years,
flayed it and dressed it, and split the whole in pieces,

323
00:22:25,280 --> 00:22:29,160
and jointed the meat with skill,
and fixed it on the spits,

324
00:22:29,160 --> 00:22:32,900
and roasted it with care, and shared out all the portions.

325
00:22:32,900 --> 00:22:35,860
So then, the whole day long until the setting of the sun,

326
00:22:35,860 --> 00:22:40,220
they feasted; and no man's appetite
went short of the equal feast.

327
00:22:40,220 --> 00:22:46,040
And when the sun went down and the dusk came on,
they lay down then, and took the gift of sleep.

328
00:22:46,040 --> 00:22:49,260
When Dawn appeared, the early-born, with fingers of rose,

329
00:22:49,260 --> 00:22:52,020
they went their way to the hunt — the hounds and with them

330
00:22:52,020 --> 00:22:55,300
the sons of Autolycus themselves; and noble Odysseus

331
00:22:55,300 --> 00:22:58,700
went along with them.
They climbed the steep mountain, mantled

332
00:22:58,700 --> 00:23:02,980
in forest — Parnassus —
and soon they reached its windy folds.

333
00:23:02,980 --> 00:23:07,920
The sun was just striking the plowlands,
rising out of the deep smooth stream of Ocean,

334
00:23:07,920 --> 00:23:13,480
when the beaters came to a glen; ahead of them ran
the hounds, casting for the scent, and behind them

335
00:23:13,480 --> 00:23:16,240
came the sons of Autolycus; and noble Odysseus

336
00:23:16,240 --> 00:23:21,100
moved close behind the hounds,
swinging his long-shadowed spear.

337
00:23:21,100 --> 00:23:24,000
There, in a dense thicket, lay couched a great boar.

338
00:23:24,000 --> 00:23:29,740
No force of wet-blowing winds could pierce that covert,
no shaft of the shining sun could strike into it,

339
00:23:29,740 --> 00:23:35,320
no rain drive through it, so dense it was,
and the fallen leaves lay heaped in it past counting.

340
00:23:35,320 --> 00:23:38,280
Around the boar came the din of feet — men's and hounds' —

341
00:23:38,280 --> 00:23:41,960
as they pressed in on him; and out of his lair, head-on,

342
00:23:41,960 --> 00:23:49,000
bristles ridged on his neck, fire standing in his eyes,
he stood at bay, close in. And first of all Odysseus

343
00:23:49,000 --> 00:23:55,160
rushed him, the long spear high in his heavy hand,
hungry to strike; but the boar was quicker — gashed him

344
00:23:55,160 --> 00:23:58,180
above the knee, charging in slantwise, and ripped away

345
00:23:58,180 --> 00:24:02,200
a long gouge of flesh with his tusk:
but it did not reach the bone.

346
00:24:02,200 --> 00:24:07,240
And Odysseus struck home on the right shoulder,
and the point of the shining spear went through and through:

347
00:24:07,240 --> 00:24:10,420
down in the dust he went, bleating,
and the life flew out of him.

348
00:24:10,420 --> 00:24:14,180
Then the dear sons of Autolycus
busied themselves with the carcass,

349
00:24:14,180 --> 00:24:20,620
and the wound of blameless, godlike Odysseus they bound
with skill, and with a chanted spell they stanched

350
00:24:20,620 --> 00:24:25,520
And Autolycus and the sons of Autolycus
healed him well, and gave him shining gifts,

351
00:24:25,520 --> 00:24:28,600
and quickly sent him back
rejoicing to his own dear country,

352
00:24:28,600 --> 00:24:31,420
to Ithaca. And his father and his honored mother

353
00:24:31,420 --> 00:24:34,700
rejoiced at his homecoming,
and questioned him on everything —

354
00:24:34,700 --> 00:24:38,660
the scar, and what had happened;
and he told them all of it well:

355
00:24:38,660 --> 00:24:44,760
how the boar's white tusk had gashed him at the hunt
on his climb up Parnassus with the sons of Autolycus.

356
00:24:44,760 --> 00:24:48,260
That scar — as the old woman took
his leg in the flat of her hands

357
00:24:48,260 --> 00:24:51,800
she knew it under her fingers. She let the foot drop:

358
00:24:51,800 --> 00:24:54,780
the shin fell in the basin; the bronze rang out;

359
00:24:54,780 --> 00:24:59,020
the basin tipped over sideways,
and the water ran on the ground.

360
00:24:59,020 --> 00:25:05,860
And joy and anguish seized her mind at once:
her eyes filled up with tears; her welling voice stuck fast.

361
00:25:05,860 --> 00:25:09,220
She touched Odysseus on the chin, and said:

362
00:25:09,220 --> 00:25:15,880
"You are Odysseus — dear child! And I did not know you,
not before I had handled all of him — my master."

363
00:25:15,880 --> 00:25:21,680
She spoke, and her eyes went toward Penelope,
ready to tell her that her own husband was in the house.

364
00:25:21,680 --> 00:25:28,200
But Penelope could not meet the look or take its meaning,
for Athena had turned her mind away. And Odysseus,

365
00:25:28,200 --> 00:25:30,760
feeling for her throat, caught it with his right hand,

366
00:25:30,760 --> 00:25:34,240
and with the other pulled her closer to him, and said:

367
00:25:34,240 --> 00:25:37,260
"Nurse — why would you destroy me? You yourself nursed me

368
00:25:37,260 --> 00:25:41,360
at this breast of yours. Now, after suffering many pains,

369
00:25:41,360 --> 00:25:44,260
I have come in the twentieth year to my fathers' land.

370
00:25:44,260 --> 00:25:47,720
But since you have found it out,
and a god has put it in your heart,

371
00:25:47,720 --> 00:25:50,720
be silent — let no one else in the halls learn of it.

372
00:25:50,720 --> 00:25:53,100
For I will say this outright, and it will be accomplished:

373
00:25:53,100 --> 00:25:55,960
if a god brings the haughty suitors down beneath my hands,

374
00:25:55,960 --> 00:26:02,120
I will not spare even you, my nurse though you are,
that day I kill the other slave-women in my halls."

375
00:26:02,120 --> 00:26:04,600
Then watchful-minded Eurycleia answered him:

376
00:26:04,600 --> 00:26:07,460
"My child, what a word has
slipped the fence of your teeth!

377
00:26:07,460 --> 00:26:10,640
You know my strength — how it stands and will not bend:

378
00:26:10,640 --> 00:26:13,340
I will hold like solid stone, like iron.

379
00:26:13,340 --> 00:26:16,420
And another thing I will tell you:
lay it up in your heart.

380
00:26:16,420 --> 00:26:19,500
If a god brings the haughty
suitors down beneath your hands,

381
00:26:19,500 --> 00:26:22,340
then I will name you the women of the halls, every one:

382
00:26:22,340 --> 00:26:25,140
which of them dishonor you, and which are guiltless."

383
00:26:25,140 --> 00:26:27,660
Then Odysseus of the many designs answered her:

384
00:26:27,660 --> 00:26:30,280
"Nurse, why would you name them? There is no need.

385
00:26:30,280 --> 00:26:33,320
I will observe them well myself, and learn each one.

386
00:26:33,320 --> 00:26:36,580
No — keep the story silent. Leave it to the gods."

387
00:26:36,580 --> 00:26:39,100
So he spoke; and the old woman went off through the hall

388
00:26:39,100 --> 00:26:42,720
to fetch fresh water for his feet:
the first was all spilled.

389
00:26:42,720 --> 00:26:48,560
And when she had washed him and rubbed him richly with oil,
Odysseus drew his bench again nearer the fire

390
00:26:48,560 --> 00:26:51,900
to warm himself, and covered the scar with his rags.

391
00:26:51,900 --> 00:26:55,200
And watchful-minded Penelope began to speak among them:

392
00:26:55,200 --> 00:27:01,100
"Stranger, one small thing more I will ask you myself —
for soon enough now comes the hour of sweet rest,

393
00:27:01,100 --> 00:27:04,240
for whomever sweet sleep will take, however careworn.

394
00:27:04,240 --> 00:27:07,000
But to me the god has dealt grief past measuring:

395
00:27:07,000 --> 00:27:09,600
by day my one delight is weeping and lamenting,

396
00:27:09,600 --> 00:27:12,880
while I look to my work and the
work of my women in the house;

397
00:27:12,880 --> 00:27:18,720
but when night comes, and rest takes all the others,
I lie in my bed, and crowding round my beating heart

398
00:27:18,720 --> 00:27:21,380
the sharp anxieties torment me as I mourn.

399
00:27:21,380 --> 00:27:24,580
As when the daughter of Pandareus,
the pale-green nightingale,

400
00:27:24,580 --> 00:27:29,560
sings beautifully at the first new standing of spring,
perched in the thick leafage of the trees,

401
00:27:29,560 --> 00:27:32,720
and pouring, turning, threads her many-noted voice,

402
00:27:32,720 --> 00:27:36,120
mourning her child, her beloved Itylus,
whom once with the bronze

403
00:27:36,120 --> 00:27:39,840
she killed in her blindness of
mind — the son of lord Zethus:

404
00:27:39,840 --> 00:27:43,080
so my heart too is torn two ways, this way and that:

405
00:27:43,080 --> 00:27:49,700
do I stay by my son and keep everything unmoved —
my goods, my slave-women, the great high-roofed house —

406
00:27:49,700 --> 00:27:52,640
respecting my husband's bed and the voice of the people;

407
00:27:52,640 --> 00:27:55,140
or do I follow now whichever of the Achaeans is best

408
00:27:55,140 --> 00:27:59,180
among my suitors in the halls,
and offers bride-gifts past counting?

409
00:27:59,180 --> 00:28:02,560
My son — while he was still a child, and soft in mind,

410
00:28:02,560 --> 00:28:05,540
he would not have me marry and leave my husband's house;

411
00:28:05,540 --> 00:28:11,240
but now that he is grown, come to the measure of manhood,
he prays me to go back home, out of these halls —

412
00:28:11,240 --> 00:28:13,820
chafing over the estate the Achaeans eat away.

413
00:28:13,820 --> 00:28:16,760
But come — hear this dream of mine, and read it for me.

414
00:28:16,760 --> 00:28:22,480
I keep twenty geese about the house; they eat wheat
softened in water, and it warms me to watch them.

415
00:28:22,480 --> 00:28:25,000
And out of the mountain came a great hook-beaked eagle

416
00:28:25,000 --> 00:28:27,940
and broke all their necks,
and killed them; and they lay strewn

417
00:28:27,940 --> 00:28:31,420
in a heap through the halls,
while he was borne up to the bright sky.

418
00:28:31,420 --> 00:28:38,880
And I wept — I wailed aloud, though it was a dream,
and around me gathered the fair-braided Achaean women,

419
00:28:38,880 --> 00:28:43,880
as I grieved most pitiably because
the eagle had killed my geese.

420
00:28:43,880 --> 00:28:50,540
But back he came, and perched on the jut of the roof-beam,
and with a man's voice he checked my grieving:

421
00:28:50,540 --> 00:28:53,240
'Take heart, daughter of far-famed Icarius:

422
00:28:53,240 --> 00:28:58,100
this is no dream but a waking sight —
a good one, and it shall be accomplished.

423
00:28:58,100 --> 00:29:01,660
The geese are the suitors; and I — I was the eagle-bird

424
00:29:01,660 --> 00:29:05,420
before; but now I have come back, and I am your husband,

425
00:29:05,420 --> 00:29:08,680
and I will let loose an ugly doom on all the suitors.'

426
00:29:08,680 --> 00:29:15,160
So he spoke; and the honey-sweet sleep let me go;
and casting about, I saw the geese there in the halls

427
00:29:15,160 --> 00:29:18,700
pecking their wheat beside the trough,
just where they always were."

428
00:29:18,700 --> 00:29:21,640
Then Odysseus of the many designs answered her:

429
00:29:21,640 --> 00:29:28,020
"Lady, there is no way to wrench this dream aside
to read it another way: Odysseus himself, no other,

430
00:29:28,020 --> 00:29:32,420
has shown you how he will end it:
destruction shows plain for the suitors —

431
00:29:32,420 --> 00:29:36,700
for every man of them; not one shall
slip out of death and the fates."

432
00:29:36,700 --> 00:29:39,380
Then watchful-minded Penelope answered him:

433
00:29:39,380 --> 00:29:43,560
"Stranger, dreams are hopeless things,
and their talk past sorting;

434
00:29:43,560 --> 00:29:46,300
not everything in them is accomplished for mortals.

435
00:29:46,300 --> 00:29:48,940
For two are the gates of strengthless dreams:

436
00:29:48,940 --> 00:29:52,040
one pair is fashioned of horn, and one of ivory.

437
00:29:52,040 --> 00:29:54,760
Those dreams that come through the gate of sawn ivory

438
00:29:54,760 --> 00:29:58,220
cheat and beguile: they carry words that come to nothing.

439
00:29:58,220 --> 00:30:00,520
But those that come out through the gate of polished horn

440
00:30:00,520 --> 00:30:03,740
accomplish true things, whenever a mortal sees them.

441
00:30:03,740 --> 00:30:10,480
But my strange dream, I fear, did not come from there —
ah, welcome as that would be to me and to my child.

442
00:30:10,480 --> 00:30:13,860
And another thing I will tell you:
lay it up in your heart.

443
00:30:13,860 --> 00:30:17,000
Here now comes the dawn of the evil name, that will cut me

444
00:30:17,000 --> 00:30:21,040
away from the house of Odysseus.
For now I will set the contest:

445
00:30:21,040 --> 00:30:27,500
the axes he used to stand up in a row in his halls,
like the props of a ship on the stocks — twelve in all;

446
00:30:27,500 --> 00:30:30,640
and standing far back he would
shoot an arrow through them.

447
00:30:30,640 --> 00:30:33,540
Now I will set this contest before the suitors:

448
00:30:33,540 --> 00:30:39,260
whoever strings the bow most easily in his hands,
and shoots an arrow through all twelve axes —

449
00:30:39,260 --> 00:30:45,460
him I will follow, forsaking this house, this house
of my marriage, this beautiful house, full of all

450
00:30:45,460 --> 00:30:49,100
that sustains life. I think I shall
remember it even in my dreams."

451
00:30:49,100 --> 00:30:52,420
Then Odysseus of the many designs answered her:

452
00:30:52,420 --> 00:30:57,660
"Honored wife of Laertes' son Odysseus,
do not put off this contest in your house one hour.

453
00:30:57,660 --> 00:31:01,120
For Odysseus of the many designs will be here first —

454
00:31:01,120 --> 00:31:07,000
before these men, handling and handling the polished bow,
can string the cord and shoot through the iron."

455
00:31:07,000 --> 00:31:09,660
Then watchful-minded Penelope answered him:

456
00:31:09,660 --> 00:31:12,320
"Stranger, if you were only
willing to sit by me in the halls

457
00:31:12,320 --> 00:31:15,480
and give me pleasure, sleep would never settle on my eyes.

458
00:31:15,480 --> 00:31:18,700
But there is no way men can go sleepless forever:

459
00:31:18,700 --> 00:31:23,380
the deathless ones have set a portion for each
thing mortals do on the grain-giving earth.

460
00:31:23,380 --> 00:31:29,300
So I will go up now to my upper room to lie on
the bed that has become a bed of groaning for me,

461
00:31:29,300 --> 00:31:36,140
forever stained with my tears, since the day Odysseus
went to look on Evil-Ilium, never to be named.

462
00:31:36,140 --> 00:31:39,420
There I will lie; and you lie down in this house —

463
00:31:39,420 --> 00:31:42,760
spread something on the floor, or let them set you a bed."

464
00:31:42,760 --> 00:31:48,540
So speaking she climbed to her shining upper room,
not alone: her handmaids went up with her.

465
00:31:48,540 --> 00:31:54,520
Climbing to her upper room with her serving-women,
she wept there for Odysseus, her dear husband, until

466
00:31:54,520 --> 00:31:57,624
gray-eyed Athena cast sweet sleep upon her eyelids.
